course: dictionary
lesson: 5136
title: Invitations and their meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An invitation is a request asking someone to come to an event, such as a party, a wedding or a meal. The word can also mean the card or message that carries the request. Context helps you choose: «Harper hands out pool-party invitations to the whole class» means the cards or messages, because you hand out things, not requests.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «Send an invitation», «accept an invitation» and «turn down an invitation» tell you that an invitation is something you can send, say yes to, or say no to. Test your meaning in the sentence: "Harper hands out requests asking people to come to a pool party" still makes sense.
explain: Invitations are part of good manners. When you receive one, you reply to say whether you can come, and you thank the person. «Rudeness often costs friendships and invitations, even when you did not mean to be rude.» So being unkind can mean you stop being asked to things.
fact: An invitation is a request asking someone to come to an event.
fact: «Invitation» can also mean the card or message that makes the request.
fact: «Accept an invitation» means say yes to it.
fact: «Turn down an invitation» means say no to it.
fact: «By invitation only» means you can come only if you are asked.
exercise: «Harper hands out pool-party invitations to the whole class.» What are the invitations? | Teachers who run the class | *Requests asking people to come to the party | Swimming costumes for the pool | Tickets for a bus
exercise: Which phrase means you say no to an invitation? | accept an invitation | *turn down an invitation | party invitation | send an invitation
exercise: «In a plan some steps must come first: the date and the place before the invitations.» What must come before the invitations? | The new home | *The date and the place | Moving day | The van
test: When you get an invitation, what should you do? | Give it to someone else | *Reply to say if you can come, and thank the person | Copy it onto the wall | Throw it away without a word
test: What does «by invitation only» mean? | Only the teachers can come | Anyone can come along | *You can come only if you are asked | You must pay to come
exam: «Rudeness often costs friendships and invitations.» What does this suggest? | *Being rude can mean people stop inviting you | Friends never mind rudeness | Invitations are only for teachers | Being rude always makes people laugh
